-- allium: 3
-- crm-gdpr.allium
--
-- GDPR / DSGVO data handling for JobSync CRM (Roadmap Sektion 5)
-- Specifies data subject rights, retention, erasure cascades, and
-- data minimization rules for CRM personal data processing.
--
-- Scope: GDPR compliance layer for CRM contact management
-- Includes: Data subject requests (access, erasure, rectification, portability),
--           consent withdrawal (Art. 7(3)), participant anonymization,
--           data classification
-- Excludes:
--   - CRM domain model (see crm.allium — imported below, NOT re-declared)
--   - Person lifecycle, anonymisation cascade and auto-created retention
--     expiry (all owned by crm.allium; this module delegates to them)
--   - Consent banner / cookie UI (not applicable — self-hosted, no tracking)
--   - Breach notification procedures (operational, not domain logic)
--   - Data Processing Agreements with vendors (legal, not code)
--   - User account deletion and full-platform export (see auth-session.allium
--     rule DeleteAccount and gdpr-data-rights.allium — the User/operator is not
--     a CRM data subject)
--
-- Legal basis: Art. 6(1)(f) DSGVO — Berechtigtes Interesse (Legitimate Interest)
-- JobSync is self-hosted, single-user/small-team. The CRM operator manages their
-- own job search contacts. No SaaS data processing agreement needed.

-- Dependencies: CRM entities come from crm.allium via the import below.
-- W-H1 (2026-08-25): this module used to hand-copy twelve CRM entities as
-- `external entity` stubs, because crm.allium did not exist when it was written.
-- It has existed since Welle 3, and the copies had drifted: they referenced
-- fields that no longer exist (Person.job_title, Person.city), missed a live
-- enum value (DataSource.quick_capture), and in one case prescribed the
-- OPPOSITE outcome to crm.allium and to the code (retention expiry: erase vs
-- archive). The instruction to migrate had been sitting in this header
-- unexecuted since Welle 3; it is now executed. Nine stubs became qualified
-- `crm/...` references; three honest externals remain (see below).
-- Do NOT re-introduce a local copy of a crm.allium entity, enum or config —
-- that is the exact mechanism this migration removed.

use "./crm.allium" as crm

-- =============================================================================
-- External Entities
-- =============================================================================

-- These three are NOT copies of anything JobSync models — they are genuine
-- externals describing an integration that does not exist yet, which is what
-- `external entity` is for. They are deliberately NOT linked to crm.allium.

external entity MessageParticipant {
    -- Email-sync participant. Modelled only in reference-twenty-crm.allium
    -- (:483) as prior art; there is no JobSync Prisma model and no code.
    --
    -- W-H1 FOLLOW-UP (2026-08-25) — the previous version of this comment said
    -- communication-connector.allium "is DEFERRED — it does not declare this
    -- entity today, so there is nothing to import". Literally true, and
    -- misleading: that spec is not a placeholder. It is a full spec with an owner
    -- line (communication-connector.allium:33), and it already models the same
    -- concept under different names — `entity MessageThread` (:123) and
    -- `entity CommunicationMessage` (:143). It is the IMPLEMENTATION that is
    -- deferred, not the specification.
    -- So: this stays external because nothing is built, NOT because the successor
    -- is unwritten. The successor to reconcile against is named above. Note the
    -- shapes do not correspond one-to-one — communication-connector reaches the
    -- contact through crm.allium's JobContact join (:50-51) and declares no
    -- per-message participant->Person link at all, so reconciling means deciding
    -- whether that link is needed, not merely renaming this entity.
    -- The consequence for erasure is recorded as an open question at the end of
    -- this file, and it is the substantive one: FulfillErasureRequest cascades
    -- over THIS entity, which nothing builds, while
    -- communication-connector.allium CommunicationMessage.body — free text about
    -- the erased person, which something eventually will build — has no erasure
    -- cascade in any spec.
    handle: String?
    person: crm/Person?
    display_name: String?
}

external entity CalendarEventParticipant {
    -- Calendar-sync participant. reference-twenty-crm.allium (:562) only, no
    -- Prisma model, no code. Stays external.
    --
    -- W-H1 FOLLOW-UP — NOT quite the same situation as MessageParticipant, and
    -- the previous comment's "same situation" elided the difference. Calendar
    -- sync has no successor spec at all: communication-connector.allium
    -- explicitly EXCLUDES it ("Calendar sync (ROADMAP 1.7, separate connector) —
    -- sync here is comms-only", :27). So this one genuinely has nothing to
    -- reconcile against yet, whereas MessageParticipant does.
    handle: String?
    person: crm/Person?
    display_name: String?
}

external entity Attachment {
    -- A file attached to a CRM contact. NOTE — this is a NAME COLLISION, not a
    -- missing import: application-documents.allium:229 also declares an
    -- `Attachment`, but that one hangs off an ApplicationBundle and has no
    -- `target_person` at all. Linking to it would be wrong. The CRM-contact
    -- attachment is modelled only in reference-twenty-crm.allium (:586) and has
    -- no Prisma model. Stays external.
    target_person: crm/Person?
}

-- =============================================================================
-- Enumerations
-- =============================================================================

-- DataSource, ProcessingBasis and BlocklistType are NOT declared here — they
-- are crm.allium's (crm/DataSource, crm/ProcessingBasis, crm/BlocklistType).
-- Local copies of all three existed until W-H1 and crm/DataSource had already
-- gained a fourth member (`quick_capture`) that the copy here never learned
-- about, leaving every rule in this file blind to a live provenance value.

enum DataSubjectRequestType {
    access         -- Art. 15: right to know what data we hold
    erasure        -- Art. 17: right to be forgotten
    rectification  -- Art. 16: right to correct inaccurate data
    portability    -- Art. 20: right to receive data in machine-readable format
}

enum DataSubjectRequestStatus {
    pending        -- received, not yet started
    processing     -- actively being fulfilled
    completed      -- fulfilled and response delivered
    rejected       -- rejected with documented reason (e.g. legal retention obligation)
}

-- DSGVO Art. 4(1) classification of CRM entities:
--   personal_data:           crm/Person, MessageParticipant, CalendarEventParticipant
--   potential_personal_data: crm/Note.body, crm/Task.description, Attachment content
--   not_personal_data:       Company (juristische Person), Opportunity, View, Workflow

-- =============================================================================
-- Value Types
-- =============================================================================

value PersonDataExport {
    -- Machine-readable export of all personal data held about a crm/Person.
    -- Produced by Art. 15 access requests and Art. 20 portability requests.
    --
    -- W-H1: this shape is now derived from what the implementation actually
    -- reads, not from the old stub. The nearest real implementation is
    -- src/lib/export/collect-user-data.ts (which serves gdpr-data-rights.allium
    -- ExportUserData — the whole-account export — and projects each Person
    -- through exactly the field set below). The previous version of this value
    -- specified `person_job_title` and `person_city`; NEITHER field exists on
    -- crm/Person any more (Welle 3 Kette B replaced job_title with `headline`
    -- and city with a structured `address`), so the spec described an export
    -- that could not be produced.
    person_name: crm/FullName
    person_emails: List<crm/TypedEmail>
    person_phones: List<crm/TypedPhone>
    person_headline: String?
    person_companies: List<crm/CompanyAssociation>
    person_social_profiles: List<crm/SocialProfile>
    person_status: crm/PersonStatus
    person_created_at: Timestamp
    data_source: crm/DataSource
    processing_basis: crm/ProcessingBasis
    consent_withdrawn_at: Timestamp?
    retention_expires_at: Timestamp?
    linked_notes: List<String>
    linked_tasks: List<String>
    linked_timeline_events: Integer
    message_threads_as_participant: Integer
    calendar_events_as_participant: Integer
    exported_at: Timestamp
}

value ErasureResult {
    -- Documents what was anonymized/removed during an Art. 17 erasure.
    -- Spans BOTH halves of the erasure (see rule FulfillErasureRequest): the
    -- person-and-CRM-links half performed by crm/AnonymizePerson, and the
    -- external-participant half performed here.
    person_anonymized: Boolean
    job_contacts_removed: Integer
    interviews_detached: Integer
    note_targets_removed: Integer
    task_targets_removed: Integer
    timeline_activities_anonymized: Integer
    message_participants_anonymized: Integer
    calendar_participants_anonymized: Integer
    attachments_removed: Integer
    retained_reason: String?
}

-- =============================================================================
-- Entities
-- =============================================================================

-- NOTE — `entity PersonGdprExtension` was DELETED by W-H1 (2026-08-25).
-- Its own comment recorded that it existed only as a workaround: "In
-- implementation: these fields are added directly to the Person Prisma model.
-- Modeled as a separate entity here because Person is an external entity from
-- the CRM reference spec." Person is no longer external, so the workaround is
-- gone and its fields live where the implementation always had them:
--   data_source, processing_basis, retention_expires_at  -> crm/Person (already)
--   is_retention_expired, is_auto_created                -> crm/Person (already)
--   consent_withdrawn_at, is_consent_blocked             -> crm/Person (added by W-H1)
--   is_anonymized                        -> crm/Person.status = anonymized
--                                           (a modelled terminal state, so the
--                                           boolean was a second source of truth)
--   erasure_requested_at                 -> DELETED: declared, never referenced
--                                           by any rule or invariant, no Prisma
--                                           column, no code. It was dead.
-- `invariant EveryPersonHasGdprExtension` went with it — it asserted a join
-- that only existed because of the split.

entity DataSubjectRequest {
    -- Tracks DSGVO data subject rights requests.
    -- Each request has a 30-day response deadline (Art. 12(3)).
    --
    -- ===== ASPIRATIONAL — NOT IMPLEMENTED =====
    -- There is NO Prisma model and NO code for this entity or for the workflow
    -- around it (`grep -rn "DataSubjectRequest" src/` returns nothing, verified
    -- 2026-08-25). Seven of the rules in this module (SubmitDataSubjectRequest,
    -- BeginProcessingRequest, FulfillAccessRequest, FulfillErasureRequest,
    -- RejectErasureForLegalRetention, FulfillRectificationRequest,
    -- FulfillPortabilityRequest) describe that unbuilt workflow. This is a
    -- deliberate forward-looking specification, not drift — but do not go
    -- looking for the table, and do not report its absence as a finding.
    -- What IS implemented today, and where:
    --   Art. 15/20 export  -> gdpr-data-rights.allium ExportUserData
    --                         (src/lib/export/collect-user-data.ts) —
    --                         whole-account, not per-data-subject
    --   Art. 17 erasure    -> crm/AnonymizePerson
    --                         (src/actions/person.actions.ts anonymizePerson)
    --   Art. 7(3) consent  -> WithdrawConsent / ReinstateConsent below
    --                         (src/actions/person.actions.ts) — see surface
    --                         ConsentManagement
    -- The gap this entity fills is the REQUEST WRAPPER: intake, the 30-day
    -- Art. 12(3) clock, status tracking and a documented rejection path.
    type: DataSubjectRequestType
    person: crm/Person
    status: DataSubjectRequestStatus
    requested_at: Timestamp
    deadline: Timestamp
    completed_at: Timestamp?
    response_data: String?
    rejection_reason: String?
    -- The CRM user who took the request into processing. Null while pending;
    -- stamped by BeginProcessingRequest, which is the only way into `processing`.
    --
    -- W-H1 FOLLOW-UP (2026-08-25) — this was `String?` and that made the erasure
    -- delegation unsound on entry. FulfillErasureRequest passes this field as the
    -- ACTOR argument of crm/AnonymizePerson, and that rule dereferences it
    -- (`requires: person.user_id = user.id`, crm.allium:654) — a bare String has
    -- no `.id`. It is typed as the User ENTITY, not as `crm/CrmUser`: an actor is
    -- a boundary role bound in a `facing` clause (the binding then carries the
    -- actor's `identified_by` entity type), not a field type. crm/CrmUser is
    -- `identified_by: User`, so crm/User is exactly what the surface binding — and
    -- therefore crm/AnonymizePerson's `user` parameter — actually is.
    processed_by_user: crm/User?

    is_overdue: status != completed and status != rejected and deadline < now

    transitions status {
        pending -> processing
        processing -> completed
        processing -> rejected
        terminal: completed, rejected
    }
}

-- =============================================================================
-- Config
-- =============================================================================

config {
    -- Art. 12(3): response deadline for data subject requests
    dsr_response_deadline: Duration = 30.days

    -- Retention window for CRM timeline activities.
    -- W-E6 named this module the owner of the timeline-retention RULE, and
    -- crm.allium:449 now points here for the value. See the CONTRADICTION note
    -- on rule ExpireOldTimelineActivities before relying on either.
    timeline_retention: Duration = 1095.days

    -- Data minimization: max DATA-SUBJECT fields captured during auto-creation.
    -- Only name + primary email; no phone, no address, no headline.
    -- The rule that enforces this is crm/AutoCreatePersonFromEmail.
    -- The value is kept here because data minimisation is this module's scope.
    --
    -- W-H1 FOLLOW-UP — this comment used to read "populates exactly these two
    -- and nothing else". That was not true, in two different ways, and only one
    -- of them is benign:
    --   benign  — the rule also stamps governance/audit fields (data_source,
    --             processing_basis, retention_expires_at, created_by, updated_by,
    --             created_at, updated_at). Those are metadata ABOUT the
    --             processing, not attributes OF the data subject, so they are
    --             outside what an allowed-fields list is for.
    --   not     — the rule ALSO seeds `companies` from the email domain, via
    --             find_company_by_domain. That is an attribute of the data
    --             subject, it is inferred rather than supplied, and it is not in
    --             this set. See the tombstone on AutoCreatedContactMinimization
    --             below and the open question at the end of this file.
    auto_creation_allowed_fields: Set<String> = {"name", "email"}
}

-- NOTE — `config auto_created_contact_retention = 730.days` was DELETED by
-- W-H1. It duplicated crm/config.auto_created_retention (same 730.days), which
-- is the value crm/AutoCreatePersonFromEmail and crm/ExpireAutoCreatedPersons
-- actually consume. Reference crm/config.auto_created_retention instead.

-- =============================================================================
-- Rules
-- =============================================================================

-- -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Data Subject Request Lifecycle
-- -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

rule SubmitDataSubjectRequest {
    -- A CRM user submits a DSR on behalf of a data subject (contact).
    -- The 30-day response clock starts immediately.
    when: SubmitRequest(person, request_type)

    requires: person != null

    ensures: DataSubjectRequest.created(
        type: request_type,
        person: person,
        status: pending,
        requested_at: now,
        deadline: now + config.dsr_response_deadline
    )
}

rule BeginProcessingRequest {
    -- Takes a pending request into processing and records WHO is processing it.
    --
    -- W-H1 FOLLOW-UP: this rule gained an actor parameter and the
    -- processed_by_user stamp. Before, NOTHING in this module ever populated
    -- processed_by_user, yet FulfillErasureRequest passed it to
    -- crm/AnonymizePerson as the acting user — so the erasure path was dead on
    -- entry. This is the rule that conceptually has the actor in hand (a human
    -- decides to start work on a request), so this is where the stamp belongs.
    -- The ownership guard is ADR-015's, restated at this boundary: only the CRM
    -- user who owns the data subject's record may process a request about it,
    -- which is also the precondition crm/AnonymizePerson will later check.
    when: BeginProcessing(user, request)

    requires: request.status = pending
    requires: request.person.user_id = user.id

    ensures: request.status = processing
    ensures: request.processed_by_user = user
}

-- -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Art. 15: Right of Access
-- -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

rule FulfillAccessRequest {
    -- Export all personal data held about the data subject.
    -- Collects data from crm/Person + all linked entities.
    when: FulfillAccess(request)

    requires: request.status = processing
    requires: request.type = access

    let person = request.person

    ensures: request.response_data = PersonDataExport(
        person_name: person.name,
        person_emails: person.emails,
        person_phones: person.phones,
        person_headline: person.headline,
        person_companies: person.companies,
        person_social_profiles: person.social_profiles,
        person_status: person.status,
        person_created_at: person.created_at,
        data_source: person.data_source,
        processing_basis: person.processing_basis,
        consent_withdrawn_at: person.consent_withdrawn_at,
        retention_expires_at: person.retention_expires_at,
        linked_notes: collect_linked_notes(person),
        linked_tasks: collect_linked_tasks(person),
        linked_timeline_events: count_timeline_activities(person),
        message_threads_as_participant: count_message_participations(person),
        calendar_events_as_participant: count_calendar_participations(person),
        exported_at: now
    )
    ensures: request.status = completed
    ensures: request.completed_at = now

    @guidance
        -- The field list mirrors the Person projection in
        -- src/lib/export/collect-user-data.ts:293-311. Fields that crm/Person
        -- holds and this payload deliberately does NOT list, because that
        -- implementation does not export them:
        --   address       -- clear Art. 15(1) gap (six Prisma columns)
        --   avatar_url    -- weaker case; a URL, possibly third-party-hosted
        --   created_by    -- ActorMetadata; Art. 15(1)(g) "information as to
        --                    their source" for a contact not collected from the
        --                    data subject. Partly covered by the exported
        --                    data_source.
        --   updated_by    -- same class, weaker
        --   updated_at    -- processing metadata; weakest case
        --
        -- W-H1 REVIEW (2026-08-26) corrected this comment: it previously said
        -- "Two fields", naming only address and avatar_url. That was wrong, and
        -- the size of the error is the point. At Prisma granularity the same
        -- gap is THIRTEEN of `model Person`'s twenty-seven scalar columns
        -- (avatarUrl, six address* columns, createdBySource, createdByName,
        -- updatedBySource, updatedByName, updatedAt, userId). Two successive
        -- careful human reviews of this hand-maintained allow-list named two of
        -- them. That is why the fix cannot be "audit the list again" — see the
        -- open question below.
        --
        -- Do not "fix" the payload here without settling that question, since
        -- spec and code would then disagree in the other direction.
}

-- -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Art. 17: Right to Erasure
-- -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

rule FulfillErasureRequest {
    -- The DSR WRAPPER around erasure. This rule deliberately does NOT restate
    -- how a Person is anonymised — crm/AnonymizePerson owns that, and owns it
    -- more completely than the copy that used to live here did (it also scrubs
    -- created_by/updated_by actor metadata, cleans up now-pointless Blocklist
    -- entries, and emits ContactDeleted).
    --
    -- The split, stated explicitly so the two halves stay fitted together:
    --   crm/AnonymizePerson  owns  the Person row itself and every CRM link
    --                              crm.allium models: interviews, note_targets,
    --                              task_targets, job_contacts, timeline
    --                              activities, blocklist cleanup.
    --   THIS rule             owns  (a) the DSR wrapper — request.status,
    --                              request.completed_at, the ErasureResult
    --                              audit record; and (b) the three
    --                              external-entity cascades crm.allium
    --                              genuinely cannot model, because it does not
    --                              declare those entities.
    -- crm.allium:675 already carries an @guidance pointing this way for exactly
    -- those three ("Implementation should also anonymize MessageParticipant and
    -- CalendarEventParticipant per crm-gdpr.allium"); this is the other end of
    -- that handshake.
    --
    -- Art. 17 is satisfied by irreversible anonymization, not row deletion: the
    -- Person row is RETAINED as a de-identified tombstone (audit trail, and it
    -- prevents silent re-creation). The user-account-deletion path in
    -- gdpr-data-rights.allium is a separate, true hard delete.
    -- CRITICAL PATTERN: anonymize participants, do NOT delete messages/events.
    -- Other participants in the same message/event still have rights to it.
    when: FulfillErasure(request)

    requires: request.status = processing
    requires: request.type = erasure
    requires: request.person.status != anonymized
    -- The delegation below hands processed_by_user to crm/AnonymizePerson as its
    -- ACTING USER, so it must actually be one. W-H1 FOLLOW-UP added this guard
    -- together with the retyping of the field and the stamp in
    -- BeginProcessingRequest; on their own the first two would leave a rule that
    -- can still be entered with a null actor.
    requires: request.processed_by_user != null

    let person = request.person

    -- (1) Delegate the whole person-and-CRM-links half.
    -- crm/AnonymizePerson's own precondition is `person.user_id = user.id`. It is
    -- discharged, not ignored: `processing` is reachable ONLY via
    -- BeginProcessingRequest (see the transition graph — pending -> processing is
    -- the sole inbound edge), and that rule requires exactly this equality before
    -- stamping the same user here. It is therefore not restated as a `requires`
    -- on this rule; if a second path into `processing` is ever added, it must
    -- carry the same guard or this reasoning breaks.
    ensures: crm/AnonymizePerson(request.processed_by_user, person)

    -- (2) Cascades crm.allium cannot express — the three external entities.

    -- Attachments on the contact are hard-deleted (they exist only for them)
    ensures:
        for a in Attachments:
            if a.target_person = person:
                not exists a

    -- Anonymize message participants (preserve the message, remove the PII link)
    ensures:
        for mp in MessageParticipants:
            if mp.person = person:
                mp.handle = null
                mp.display_name = null
                mp.person = null

    -- Anonymize calendar participants (same pattern, same reason)
    ensures:
        for cp in CalendarEventParticipants:
            if cp.person = person:
                cp.handle = null
                cp.display_name = null
                cp.person = null

    -- (3) Record what was done, across both halves, for the audit trail.
    ensures: request.response_data = ErasureResult(
        person_anonymized: true,
        job_contacts_removed: person.job_contacts -> count,
        interviews_detached: person.interviews -> count,
        note_targets_removed: person.note_targets -> count,
        task_targets_removed: person.task_targets -> count,
        timeline_activities_anonymized: person.timeline_activities -> count,
        message_participants_anonymized: count(MessageParticipants, mp => mp.person = person),
        calendar_participants_anonymized: count(CalendarEventParticipants, cp => cp.person = person),
        attachments_removed: count(Attachments, a => a.target_person = person),
        retained_reason: null
    )
    ensures: request.status = completed
    ensures: request.completed_at = now

    @guidance
        -- The counts must be evaluated BEFORE the cascades run, since the
        -- cascades are what make them zero.
}

rule RejectErasureForLegalRetention {
    -- Erasure can be refused if there is a legal obligation to retain.
    -- Art. 17(3)(b): compliance with a legal obligation.
    -- Example: tax-relevant correspondence within retention period.
    when: RejectErasure(request, reason)

    requires: request.status = processing
    requires: request.type = erasure

    ensures: request.status = rejected
    ensures: request.rejection_reason = reason
    ensures: request.completed_at = now
}

-- -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Art. 16: Right to Rectification
-- -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

rule FulfillRectificationRequest {
    -- Update crm/Person fields with corrected data.
    -- After rectification, re-run participant matching to update
    -- any MessageParticipant/CalendarEventParticipant links.
    when: FulfillRectification(request, corrected_fields)

    requires: request.status = processing
    requires: request.type = rectification

    ensures: apply_corrections(request.person, corrected_fields)
    ensures: rematch_participants(request.person)
    ensures: request.status = completed
    ensures: request.completed_at = now
}

-- -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Art. 20: Right to Data Portability
-- -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

rule FulfillPortabilityRequest {
    -- Export personal data in a structured, machine-readable format (JSON).
    -- Same data collection as the access request, but formatted for transfer.
    when: FulfillPortability(request)

    requires: request.status = processing
    requires: request.type = portability

    let person = request.person

    ensures: request.response_data = PersonDataExport(
        person_name: person.name,
        person_emails: person.emails,
        person_phones: person.phones,
        person_headline: person.headline,
        person_companies: person.companies,
        person_social_profiles: person.social_profiles,
        person_status: person.status,
        person_created_at: person.created_at,
        data_source: person.data_source,
        processing_basis: person.processing_basis,
        consent_withdrawn_at: person.consent_withdrawn_at,
        retention_expires_at: person.retention_expires_at,
        linked_notes: collect_linked_notes(person),
        linked_tasks: collect_linked_tasks(person),
        linked_timeline_events: count_timeline_activities(person),
        message_threads_as_participant: count_message_participations(person),
        calendar_events_as_participant: count_calendar_participations(person),
        exported_at: now
    )
    ensures: request.status = completed
    ensures: request.completed_at = now
}

-- -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Retention Enforcement
-- -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

-- NOTE — `rule ExpireAutoCreatedContacts` was DELETED by W-H1 (2026-08-25).
-- crm.allium rule ExpireAutoCreatedPersons is the SOLE OWNER of auto-created
-- retention expiry. Do NOT re-add a copy here.
--
-- W-H1 deleted it because the two modules CONTRADICTED each other on the same
-- trigger and the same subject: this module said expiry ends in `anonymized`
-- (terminal, irreversible), crm.allium said `archived` (reversible) plus a
-- ReminderTriggered, and the code archived. `allium check` could not see the
-- conflict, because one spec talked about Person and the other about
-- PersonGdprExtension. A reader consulting the GDPR spec — the natural place to
-- look for a retention answer — was told erasure happened when it did not.
--
-- W-B3 UPDATE (2026-08-26) — READ THIS BEFORE CONCLUDING THE DELETION WAS WRONG.
-- The two outcomes have since CONVERGED on `anonymized`: crm.allium
-- ExpireAutoCreatedPersons now delegates to AnonymizePerson, and the code erases
-- (src/lib/scheduler/crm-cron.ts calls anonymizePersonCascade with
-- reason: retention_expired). It no longer archives, no longer writes a
-- retention_expired timeline entry, and no longer emits ReminderTriggered; the
-- selector is now `{status: {not: "anonymized"}, dataSource: "auto_created",
-- retentionExpiresAt: {lte: now}}`, so manually-archived records no longer
-- escape. The description two paragraphs above of what the code does is
-- therefore HISTORICAL — it described the pre-W-B3 implementation.
--
-- So the outcome this module originally prescribed turned out to be the right
-- one. That does NOT make the deletion wrong, and is exactly the reasoning trap
-- this note exists to stop: W-H1 removed a SECOND OWNER, not a wrong answer. Two
-- modules independently specifying one rule is a drift mechanism whichever of
-- them happens to be correct at any moment — it is what allowed the divergence
-- to persist unseen in the first place. Ownership stays with crm.allium.

rule ExpireOldTimelineActivities {
    -- Periodic cleanup: timeline activities older than the retention window
    -- have their person reference removed (the row survives, de-identified).
    --
    -- ===== CONTRADICTION — UNRESOLVED, DO NOT TRUST THIS RULE ALONE =====
    -- W-H1 (2026-08-25) found that this rule disagrees with both the code and
    -- another spec, in the same way ExpireAutoCreatedContacts did:
    --   THIS rule                              anonymise in place
    --                                          (`ta.target_person = null`)
    --   gdpr-data-rights.allium:404            hard DELETE the row
    --   PurgeOldCrmActivityLogs                (`not exists cal`)
    --   src/lib/scheduler/retention-cron.ts    hard DELETE the row
    --   :203 purgeOldCrmActivityLogs           (`prisma.crmActivityLog
    --                                            .deleteMany`)
    -- Same subject (CRM timeline entries), same window (1095 days), different
    -- outcome. The code and gdpr-data-rights.allium agree with each other; this
    -- rule is the outlier, so on current evidence it is the one that is wrong.
    -- It was NOT deleted here only because fixing it properly means choosing a
    -- single owner across a spec this work stream is not scoped to edit
    -- (gdpr-data-rights.allium). See the open question at the end of this file.
    when: RunRetentionCleanup()

    ensures:
        for ta in crm/ActivityLogs:
            if ta.happened_at + config.timeline_retention < now:
                ta.target_person = null

    @guidance
        -- W-H1 also corrected the field name: the old local stub called this
        -- `happens_at`, but crm/ActivityLog (and Prisma) call it `happened_at`.
}

-- -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Art. 7(3): Consent Withdrawal
-- -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

rule WithdrawConsent {
    -- Art. 7(3): the data subject may withdraw consent at any time, as easily
    -- as it was given. Only meaningful for consent-based records, and only when
    -- consent has not already been withdrawn. The withdrawal restricts further
    -- processing (see invariant ConsentBlockedRecordIsProcessingRestricted);
    -- processing performed before this point stays lawful. Reversible via
    -- ReinstateConsent.
    when: WithdrawConsent(user, person)

    requires: person.user_id = user.id
    requires: person.processing_basis = consent
    requires: person.consent_withdrawn_at = null

    ensures: person.consent_withdrawn_at = now
    ensures: person.updated_by = crm/ActorMetadata(source: manual, name: user.name)
    -- Emitted BARE, not as `crm/ContactUpdated`. W-H1 first wrote the qualified
    -- form by analogy with crm/ActorMetadata above, but the two are not alike:
    -- ActorMetadata is a `value` DECLARED in crm.allium, whereas ContactUpdated
    -- is a trigger no spec declares — crm.allium emits it bare (UpdatePerson,
    -- AddJobContact, RemoveJobContact) and consumes it bare (RecordContactUpdate),
    -- and its payload shape is event-bus.allium `value ContactUpdatedPayload`
    -- {person_id, user_id}. A qualified name with nothing to resolve to is a
    -- dangling reference; `allium check` cannot see it, scripts/check-spec-refs.mjs
    -- can. Bare emission also keeps this rule chaining into crm.allium's timeline
    -- projection, which is the behaviour the code has (person.actions.ts
    -- withdrawConsent publishes contact_updated).
    ensures: ContactUpdated(person_id: person.id, user_id: user.id)

    @guidance
        -- Implemented by withdrawConsent(personId) in src/actions/person.actions.ts:
        -- ownership-scoped findFirst (ADR-015), rejects a non-consent basis with
        -- crm.errors.consentNotApplicable and an already-withdrawn record with
        -- crm.errors.consentAlreadyWithdrawn, then writes consentWithdrawnAt +
        -- updatedBySource/updatedByName and publishes ContactUpdated.
}

rule ReinstateConsent {
    -- Inverse of WithdrawConsent: the data subject re-grants consent, clearing
    -- the withdrawal marker and lifting the processing restriction. Only valid
    -- for a consent-based record that is currently withdrawn.
    when: ReinstateConsent(user, person)

    requires: person.user_id = user.id
    requires: person.processing_basis = consent
    requires: person.consent_withdrawn_at != null

    ensures: person.consent_withdrawn_at = null
    ensures: person.updated_by = crm/ActorMetadata(source: manual, name: user.name)
    -- Bare, for the reason given on WithdrawConsent above.
    ensures: ContactUpdated(person_id: person.id, user_id: user.id)

    @guidance
        -- Implemented by reinstateConsent(personId) in person.actions.ts; the
        -- mirror of the above, rejecting a not-withdrawn record with
        -- crm.errors.consentNotWithdrawn.
}

-- -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Data Minimization at Contact Creation
-- -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

-- NOTE — `rule AutoCreatedContactMinimization` and
-- `rule ManuallyCreatedContactMetadata` were DELETED by W-H1 (2026-08-25).
-- Both existed only to stamp data_source / processing_basis /
-- retention_expires_at onto the old PersonGdprExtension. crm.allium already
-- sets exactly those fields, on the Person itself, at the creating rule:
--   auto-created  -> crm/AutoCreatePersonFromEmail (data_source: auto_created,
--                    processing_basis: legitimate_interest,
--                    retention_expires_at: now + config.auto_created_retention)
--   manual        -> crm/CreatePerson (data_source: manual,
--                    processing_basis: legitimate_interest, no retention limit)
--   quick capture -> crm/QuickCapturePerson (data_source: quick_capture,
--                    processing_basis: legitimate_interest, NO retention limit
--                    — see the open question below; this module was asked to
--                    decide and has not been able to)
-- Keeping second copies here is what let the retention contradiction survive
-- unnoticed for two Wellen.
--
-- W-H1 FOLLOW-UP (2026-08-25) — WHAT THE DELETION ALSO TOOK, recorded so the
-- change set's thesis ("lose no constraint") stays honest. The justification
-- above accounts only for the three fields AutoCreatedContactMinimization
-- STAMPED. That rule also carried a PROHIBITION, in prose: "No phone, no city,
-- no job_title, no enrichment." crm/AutoCreatePersonFromEmail discharges most of
-- it structurally — it creates `phones: {}` and sets no address and no headline,
-- which is stronger than prose — but NOT the last clause: it derives
-- `let matching_company = find_company_by_domain(user.id, domain)` and, on a
-- hit, seeds `companies` with a CompanyAssociation (crm.allium:586-590). That is
-- an inferred attribute about the data subject, i.e. exactly the enrichment the
-- deleted prose forbade.
-- Assessed as PRE-EXISTING crm.allium behaviour, LOW severity, and deliberately
-- NOT changed here: the inference is domain-derived rather than looked up from a
-- third party, and it is what makes an auto-created contact useful at all. But
-- it is now WRITTEN DOWN rather than silently dropped, and the config comment on
-- auto_creation_allowed_fields (which used to claim the rule "populates exactly
-- these two and nothing else") has been corrected to match. See the open
-- question at the end of this file: someone has to decide whether the
-- prohibition or the behaviour is the thing that should change.

rule ImportedContactMetadata {
    -- ===== ASPIRATIONAL — NO IMPLEMENTATION =====
    -- When a Person is imported (CSV, API, etc.), it is governed like an
    -- auto-created contact: same 2-year retention leash, legitimate interest.
    -- Unlike the two rules deleted above, this one has NO counterpart in
    -- crm.allium — there is no import rule there — so deleting it would lose
    -- the only statement of how imported contacts are governed.
    -- `imported` IS a live crm/DataSource value and the UI can already display
    -- it (src/models/person.model.ts, ContactsPageClient.tsx), but nothing in
    -- the codebase creates a Person with it. This rule specifies a path that
    -- has not been built. When bulk import (ROADMAP 5.7/5.8) lands, this rule
    -- should move to crm.allium next to CreatePerson, and this module should
    -- keep only the retention judgement.
    when: ImportPerson(user, person)

    ensures: person.data_source = imported
    ensures: person.processing_basis = legitimate_interest
    ensures: person.retention_expires_at = now + crm/config.auto_created_retention
}

-- =============================================================================
-- Invariants
-- =============================================================================

-- NOTE — `invariant EveryPersonHasGdprExtension` was DELETED by W-H1: it
-- asserted the Person <-> PersonGdprExtension join, which only existed because
-- Person was an external stub. With the fields on crm/Person and non-null by
-- declaration, the property is structural and needs no invariant.
--
-- NOTE — `invariant AutoCreatedContactHasRetention` was DELETED by W-H1: it is
-- exactly crm/Person's own `invariant AutoCreatedHasRetention`
-- (`data_source = auto_created implies retention_expires_at != null`), which
-- lives on the entity where it belongs.

invariant DataSubjectRequestHasDeadline {
    for r in DataSubjectRequests:
        r.deadline != null
}

invariant DataSubjectRequestDeadlineWithin30Days {
    -- Art. 12(3): response within one month of receipt
    for r in DataSubjectRequests:
        r.deadline <= r.requested_at + config.dsr_response_deadline
}

invariant ErasedPersonIsFullyAnonymized {
    -- After erasure, no MessageParticipant or CalendarEventParticipant
    -- should still reference the erased Person.
    for mp in MessageParticipants:
        mp.person != null implies exists p in crm/Persons where p = mp.person

    for cp in CalendarEventParticipants:
        cp.person != null implies exists p in crm/Persons where p = cp.person
}

invariant ConsentBlockOnlyOnConsentBasis {
    -- Art. 7(3): consent withdrawal only restricts records processed on the
    -- consent basis. A withdrawal marker on a legitimate_interest or contract
    -- record would be meaningless — those bases are unaffected by withdrawal.
    for p in crm/Persons:
        p.consent_withdrawn_at != null implies p.processing_basis = consent
}

invariant ConsentBlockedRecordIsProcessingRestricted {
    -- Art. 7(3): once consent is withdrawn, the Person record MUST NOT be
    -- processed further. Concretely: no field edits and no automated activity
    -- (e.g. interview reminders) may act on a consent-blocked record, and no
    -- NEW link that names the record may be created — this includes adding a
    -- job contact (AddJobContact) and recording a network edge on either
    -- endpoint (AddPersonConnection), alongside note/task/interview/referral
    -- creation and the warm-path and cron flows. The only operations that
    -- remain permitted are export (Art. 15 access / Art. 20 portability),
    -- erasure or anonymization (Art. 17), and consent reinstatement (which
    -- lifts the restriction). Processing that occurred before withdrawal stays
    -- lawful and is not undone — hence this is a guard on the creating ACTION,
    -- not a standing predicate over existing links.
    --
    -- THIS INVARIANT HAS NO PREDICATE, DELIBERATELY. Read the next paragraph
    -- before adding one back.
    --
    -- W-H1 REVIEW (2026-08-26) — it used to carry
    --     for p in crm/Persons:
    --         p.is_consent_blocked implies p.processing_basis = consent
    -- which asserts NOTHING. crm/Person.is_consent_blocked is DEFINED as
    -- `processing_basis = consent and consent_withdrawn_at != null`
    -- (crm.allium:295), so the expression unfolds to
    -- `(A and B) implies A` = true, for every Person, always. It was a
    -- degenerate restatement of the sibling invariant
    -- ConsentBlockOnlyOnConsentBasis above, which says the same thing in its
    -- non-vacuous form (`consent_withdrawn_at != null implies
    -- processing_basis = consent`) and is genuinely upheld — crm/UpdatePerson
    -- requires `not person.is_consent_blocked`, so once withdrawn the basis
    -- cannot be edited away. Two invariants, one invariant's worth of content,
    -- and the weaker one produced a FALSE GREEN: `allium check` reported no
    -- error and a reader concluded the Art. 7(3) restriction was machine-
    -- checked. It never was. That is the same defect class W-H1 exists to
    -- remove, one level up: a construct that LOOKS verified and is not.
    --
    -- The property above is not expressible as an invariant, and the language
    -- reference says so directly. `invariant Name { expr }` asserts "properties
    -- over entity state at a single point in time"; TEMPORAL ORDERING and
    -- guards on actions are listed under "Not expressible — use prose comments
    -- or @invariant in contracts", with the closing rule "If it requires
    -- comparing two moments in time [...] it belongs in prose."
    -- "Processing performed before withdrawal stays lawful and is not undone"
    -- is exactly a two-moment comparison. So prose is not a concession here; it
    -- is the prescribed form.
    --
    -- The construct and its NAME are kept rather than deleted because the name
    -- is load-bearing: crm.allium cites it six times (:294, :623, :960, :1094,
    -- :1605, :1862) and it is the only written statement anywhere of WHAT the
    -- restriction forbids. The enforcement now genuinely exists, as `requires`
    -- guards on the rules themselves (crm.allium:627, :791, :847, :937, :982,
    -- :1074, :1115) — which is why the filler predicate is no longer even
    -- serving as a placeholder for absent enforcement.
    --
    -- The structural half is checked, non-vacuously, by
    -- ConsentBlockOnlyOnConsentBasis above. Do not duplicate it here.
    --
    -- If a future allium release requires a non-empty invariant body, this
    -- becomes a parse error — i.e. it fails LOUDLY, which is the intended
    -- trade. Do not silence it by reinstating a tautology; convert it to an
    -- @invariant annotation on surface ConsentManagement instead.
}

invariant BlocklistSuppressesAutoCreation {
    -- No Person should be auto-created for a handle suppressed by the blocklist.
    -- Enforced at creation time, not as a retroactive check. A crm/Blocklist
    -- entry suppresses according to its `type` (Welle 3 Gap-6):
    --   email | phone → exact handle match
    --   domain        → the email's domain or any parent domain
    --   pattern       → a `*`-glob over the handle (ReDoS-safe)
    -- Only the exact (email) case is expressible as a pure set predicate here;
    -- the domain-suffix and glob modes are defined by the blocklist matching
    -- rules and enforced by the same suppression check.
    -- SOLE OWNER of this property (weed W-F1, 2026-08-17). crm.allium carried a
    -- duplicate of this invariant under the same name; the two diverged into
    -- opposite enforcement models (retroactive vs creation-time) before anyone
    -- noticed. The duplicate has been removed: auto-creation constraints and
    -- data-minimisation rules are this spec's declared scope, and only this
    -- version documents all four match modes. crm.allium enforces the property
    -- operationally, through AutoCreatePersonFromEmail's `requires` guards.
    --
    -- W-H1 — the quick_capture case, stated rather than left silent: this
    -- invariant guards `is_auto_created` only, i.e. data_source = auto_created.
    -- A crm/QuickCapturePerson (data_source = quick_capture) is therefore NOT
    -- suppressed by the blocklist, and crm/QuickCapturePerson declares no
    -- blocklist `requires` either — so a user CAN quick-capture a blocklisted
    -- handle. Assessed as CORRECT and left as-is: the blocklist suppresses
    -- AUTO-creation (an unattended sync deciding to store a person), and a quick
    -- capture is a deliberate, attended user act against a handle the user has
    -- typed. Widening this to all data sources would let a stale blocklist entry
    -- silently block a contact the user is explicitly trying to create. The
    -- retention half of the quick_capture question is a different matter and is
    -- genuinely undecided — see the open question at the end of this file.
    for p in crm/Persons:
        for b in crm/Blocklists:
            p.is_auto_created and b.type = email
                implies not p.emails.any(e => e.email = b.handle)
}

-- =============================================================================
-- Actors
-- =============================================================================

-- This module declares NO actor of its own. It reuses crm.allium's CrmUser
-- (crm.allium `actor CrmUser { identified_by: User }`) by qualified reference,
-- because the data subject's record IS a crm/Person and the person acting on it
-- IS the CRM user who owns it (crm/Person.user_id, ADR-015).
-- Declaring a local `actor CrmUser` here would re-create, at the actor level,
-- exactly the copy-paste drift that W-H1 removed at the entity level.

-- =============================================================================
-- Surfaces
-- =============================================================================

surface ConsentManagement {
    -- W-H1 (2026-08-25) — THE FIX. Until now this module declared zero actors
    -- and zero surfaces, so WithdrawConsent and ReinstateConsent — two Art. 7(3)
    -- actions that are genuinely user-facing, invoked from
    -- src/app/dashboard/contacts/[id]/PersonDetailClient.tsx:140,150 — existed
    -- at no declared boundary in ANY spec. That was not an oversight: the
    -- mechanism was the stub. The old `external entity Person` had no `user_id`,
    -- so there was no ownership predicate, so no actor could be tied to it, so
    -- no surface could be declared. Importing crm.allium supplies both
    -- crm/Person.user_id and crm/CrmUser, and the boundary becomes declarable.
    facing user: crm/CrmUser
    context person: crm/Person where user_id = user.id

    exposes:
        person.processing_basis
        person.consent_withdrawn_at
        person.is_consent_blocked

    provides:
        WithdrawConsent(user, person)
            when person.processing_basis = consent
                and person.consent_withdrawn_at = null
        ReinstateConsent(user, person)
            when person.processing_basis = consent
                and person.consent_withdrawn_at != null

    related:
        crm/PersonDetail(person)

    @guidance
        -- Not a standalone screen: rendered inside crm.allium's PersonDetail
        -- surface, in the GDPR/governance area alongside the data_source /
        -- processing_basis / retention_expires_at fields PersonDetail already
        -- exposes. Both actions are confirm-dialog-gated in the implementation.
        -- Both are ownership-scoped (ADR-015) and both are no-ops on a
        -- non-consent basis, which is why the `when` guards mirror the rules'
        -- `requires` exactly rather than merely hiding the buttons.
}

-- =============================================================================
-- Open Questions
-- =============================================================================

-- RESOLVED — W-H1 (2026-08-25). This slot used to hold an open question asking
-- where WithdrawConsent / ReinstateConsent belong, given that this module
-- declared no actor and no surface while its Person was an external stub. It is
-- answered, not abandoned: the stub is gone, this module imports crm.allium,
-- and the two Art. 7(3) actions are now declared on `surface ConsentManagement`
-- above, facing crm/CrmUser and contexted on crm/Person where user_id = user.id.
-- The question's own predicted remedy ("once the documented use \"./crm.allium\"
-- as crm migration lands it can borrow crm's CrmUser actor and expose a
-- ConsentManagement surface") is exactly what was done. Do not re-raise it.

open question "W-H1 FOLLOW-UP — auto-creation enrichment: the deleted rule AutoCreatedContactMinimization forbade enrichment in prose (\"No phone, no city, no job_title, no enrichment\"), and config.auto_creation_allowed_fields still says {name, email}. But crm/AutoCreatePersonFromEmail seeds a CompanyAssociation inferred from the email domain (find_company_by_domain, crm.allium:586-590), which is an inferred attribute of the data subject and is not in that set. Only one of the two can stand. Either (a) the inference is acceptable data minimisation under Art. 5(1)(c) — it is derived from the handle the data subject themselves used, not looked up from a third party — in which case widen auto_creation_allowed_fields to {name, email, company} and retire the prohibition explicitly rather than by deletion; or (b) it is not, in which case crm/AutoCreatePersonFromEmail must stop seeding companies. W-H1 FOLLOW-UP deliberately did not choose: this is pre-existing shipped behaviour in another module\'s rule, and the change set that surfaced it was scoped to comments and references. Whoever chooses should note that (b) is a behaviour change to auto-creation, and that the same question does not arise for crm/QuickCapturePerson or crm/CreatePerson, where the user supplies the company."

open question "W-H1 FOLLOW-UP — the erasure cascade covers entities nobody builds and misses the one that will hold the text. FulfillErasureRequest anonymises MessageParticipant and CalendarEventParticipant, neither of which has a Prisma model, code, or a successor declaration. Meanwhile communication-connector.allium — a written spec, owner line at :33, implementation deferred — declares entity MessageThread (:123) and entity CommunicationMessage (:143), and CommunicationMessage carries `body: String`: free text exchanged with, and about, the person being erased. NO spec declares an erasure cascade over it. That is a real Art. 17 gap the moment the Communication Connector ships, and it is a design decision, not an oversight to patch here, because a message body is jointly the erased person\'s data AND the CRM user\'s own correspondence — the same tension already parked in the crm/Note.body / crm/Task.description question above, and it should probably be settled with that one. Two sub-decisions: (1) does the cascade anonymise in place (consistent with the CRITICAL PATTERN this module already states — other participants retain rights to the same message) or redact the body; and (2) which spec owns it — communication-connector.allium, which declares the entity, or this module, which owns erasure. Note also that communication-connector reaches the contact via crm.allium\'s JobContact join (:50-51) and declares NO per-message participant->Person link, so there is currently no traversal from an erased Person to their messages at all; a cascade needs that link to exist first. Deciding this also determines whether MessageParticipant survives as an entity or is superseded."

open question "Should auto-created contacts receive a notification that their data is being processed? Art. 14 requires informing data subjects when data is obtained indirectly — but self-hosted single-user CRM may qualify for the household exemption (Art. 2(2)(c))."

open question "Should the retention period for imported contacts differ from auto-created? Imports are deliberate user actions, auto-creation is automatic. Consider: imported = no auto-expiry (like manual), or same 2-year TTL? Note rule ImportedContactMetadata currently assumes the same 2-year TTL, and is aspirational — no import path exists yet, so this is still cheap to change."

open question "Should crm/Note.body and crm/Task.description free-text fields be included in erasure cascades? They MAY contain personal data about the erased person but also contain the CRM user's own notes. Consider: flag-and-review vs automatic inclusion in export, exclude from deletion."

open question "Art. 15 completeness: PersonDataExport omits FIVE crm/Person fields — address, avatar_url, created_by, updated_by and updated_at (W-H1 REVIEW 2026-08-26 corrected an earlier version of this question, which named only the first two). This is not an oversight in the spec — it mirrors the implementation exactly (src/lib/export/collect-user-data.ts:295-310 selects firstName, lastName, headline, emails, phones, companies, socialProfiles, status, dataSource, processingBasis, consentWithdrawnAt, retentionExpiresAt, createdAt, and nothing else; all six address* columns, avatarUrl, createdBySource, createdByName, updatedBySource, updatedByName and updatedAt are absent — THIRTEEN of the model\'s twenty-seven scalar columns are unexported, not two). W-H1 aligned the spec to the code rather than silently widening the payload, because the two directions have different consequences: widening the SPEC alone would re-create drift, and widening the CODE is a behaviour change to a live GDPR export. Art. 15(1) entitles the data subject to all personal data undergoing processing, and a postal address plainly qualifies, so the likely answer is that the CODE should grow, not that the spec should stay narrow. avatar_url is the weaker case (a URL, possibly to an image of the data subject, possibly to a third-party-hosted asset). created_by/updated_by are a third case nobody has raised: Art. 15(1)(g) entitles a data subject whose data was not collected from them to any available information as to its source, and for an auto-created contact created_by IS that information (partly, but only partly, covered by the exported data_source). Settle them together and change spec and code in the same commit. AND -- the more important half -- do not settle this by auditing the list, because auditing the list is what has already failed twice: the payload is a hand-maintained allow-list, and two successive careful reviews named two of the thirteen unexported columns. The durable fix is a test asserting that every scalar column on Prisma model Person is either exported or named in a documented exclusion set, so that adding a column forces a decision. Scope that test at FIELD level over the models ALREADY in collect-user-data.ts, not at model level: the model list has in fact been maintained (Welle 5 added Referral and PersonConnection when those models landed), whereas columns added to a model already in the list -- ROADMAP 1.21 put addressCountryCode and addressSubdivisionCode on Person long after the export existed -- are invisible to whoever edits the export. Adding a model prompts the question; adding a column does not."

open question "W-H1 FOLLOW-UP — retention governance for quick_capture Persons is STILL UNDECIDED, and this module is where crm.allium (open question at crm.allium:1749) deferred the decision. Now that crm/DataSource is imported rather than hand-copied, the value is at least VISIBLE here — the old local copy of the enum had only three members and could not express it. State of play: crm/QuickCapturePerson sets no retention_expires_at, crm/Person.AutoCreatedHasRetention constrains only data_source = auto_created, and crm/ExpireAutoCreatedPersons guards on is_auto_created — so a quick_capture Person is today governed exactly like a full manual create (no expiry, ever). Is that intended? Quick capture is a deliberate user act (unlike email-sync auto-creation), which argues for manual-like treatment; but the record is knowingly incomplete at birth and may be a contact the user never returns to, which argues for a shorter leash. This needs a product/legal judgement, not a spec edit, so W-H1 deliberately did NOT settle it. Whoever settles it must also decide whether ImportedContactMetadata's sibling question (above) gets the same answer. Decide before quick-capture volume accumulates: retro-fitting an expiry onto existing records is a data-minimisation judgement made after the fact."

open question "W-H1 REVIEW (2026-08-26) -- the stub mechanism this module just removed is STILL LIVE in three other specs, and one of them is the file two of the questions above hand work to. Grepping specs/ for external-entity Person declarations returns four files; the three besides this one declare no use line at all. (1) gdpr-data-rights.allium declares external entity Person { user: User, name: String?, emails: List<String> } -- ALL THREE fields diverge from crm/Person (user_id: String, name: FullName, emails: List<TypedEmail>), with no comment explaining the divergence. Same stale-shape class as the stub deleted from here, in the module that owns CrmActivityLog retention (its own Scope line S4) and the Art. 15 export -- i.e. the file in which both the timeline-ownership question and the Art. 15 question will be resolved. Fix that stub BEFORE either, or the resolution gets written against a shape that is already wrong. (2) audit-trail.allium declares external entity Person { user: User } and documents WHY it mirrors no PII (the audit trail records ACCESS to them, never copies their content) -- that is a good stub; it needs only a user vs user_id alignment. (3) application-documents.allium declares external entity Person { gender: String?, full_name: FullName? }, and gender exists NOWHERE -- not in crm.allium, not in prisma/schema.prisma, not in src/models/person.model.ts -- yet that spec builds a salutation feature on it (Salutation.resolve considering Person.gender at :403, enum member formal_named at :115, an invariant at :541). Different class: not stale drift but a REQUIREMENT smuggled into a stub, benign today only because that whole spec is aspirational (Owner: ... to build). It will surface as \"Person has no gender\" the day ROADMAP 4.2 starts. Whoever schedules the next W-item should take gdpr-data-rights.allium first, on exactly the argument that justified flipping this module: the defect is the mechanism, not the instance."

open question "W-H1 FOUND A SECOND CONTRADICTION, out of scope to fix here — timeline-activity retention has two specs and they disagree. This module's rule ExpireOldTimelineActivities anonymises in place (target_person = null, row survives); gdpr-data-rights.allium:404 PurgeOldCrmActivityLogs hard-deletes the row; and the implementation (src/lib/scheduler/retention-cron.ts:203 purgeOldCrmActivityLogs, prisma.crmActivityLog.deleteMany) hard-deletes. Both specs use the same 1095-day window, from two separately-declared config values (this module's timeline_retention and gdpr-data-rights' crm_activity_log_retention). Code and gdpr-data-rights.allium agree, so on current evidence THIS module's rule is the wrong one. Resolving it properly means naming a single owner and deleting the loser, which requires editing gdpr-data-rights.allium — outside the W-H1 mandate (crm.allium + crm-gdpr.allium only). Note the two rules are not merely redundant: a hard delete also destroys the non-personal audit value of the timeline entry, so 'which is correct' is a real GDPR judgement (Art. 5(1)(e) storage limitation vs Art. 5(1)(f)/accountability), not just bookkeeping. NOTE for whoever picks this up: W-E6's earlier resolution — 'the timeline-retention RULE lives in crm-gdpr.allium' — was reached by comparing this module against crm.allium ONLY, and did not consider gdpr-data-rights.allium at all. Re-open that decision rather than inheriting it."
